I was hanging out with the cool kids in rec.games.roguelike.nethack when 
Satanicus got out a spraycan and scrawled the following:
> > From: dogsc...@eudoramail.com
> > So here's the thread's question: Do you remember your first ever wish?
> > Was it from a \,/,( or &? Were you unspoiled at the time? Did you ask
> > for something you'd already seen in nethack, or something you'd only
> > read about or guessed at?

It was a water daemon, and I'd heard tell of such great things as dragon
scale mails.  However, I didn't know what colour was the best, or even
whether it was "scales of dragon" or some such, so I just asked for
"mail".  I got a stamped scroll.

> [snip]
>  The thought, in turn, inspires *me* to inquire about everyone else's
>  experiences prior to their first spoiler: playing styles and concepts
>  that seem utterly stupid today?

Well, of course I ignored the Guidebook completely, and went bashing
everything and promptly dying.  I thought that shops were the only way
to get decent stuff, so I kept carrying all dropped items to the nearest
shopkeeper in order to gain a bit of extra cash.

After reading the Guidebook, I always went for the mines before the rest
of the dungeon, because I knew that they were a dead end and didn't want
to waste time going further into the main dungeon when I'd still have to
come back up and finish them.  The few characters that got beyond the
town tended to die to gargoyles or polymorphed nasties.

> Favorite thing discovered on your own
>  (for me it was giving the *ahem* "right" answer to a vault guard)?

I can think of very few "I wonder if that will work..." situations where
I turned out to be right.  I think I sussed the boulder-in-pit method of
removing that iron ball shortly after my first visit to Sokoban.  (I was
also pleased to see "The rust monster eats an iron chain", although that
was through no effort or reasoning of my own.)  Guessing what the
expensive camera was good for allowed one of my early tourists to live a
few moves longer.

>  How,
>  now that we're on the subject, did you stumble across RGRN/spoilers in
>  the first place?

Like Adam blaming his sins on Eve, I can blame this on my other half.  I
introduced her to this fascinating little timewaster I'd found on
my shiny new Debian CD (potato), then she got frustrated and went and
got herself spoiled and promptly ascended.  (She did discover dipping-
for-Excalibur by typo, though.)  I started picking up all the important
tips from her - the uses of prayer and holy water, monsters on the
stairway, unicorn horns, stuff that every winner should carry...

The first time I actually searched for spoilers was when I had to do my
first invocation - I lit the light and read the book and rang the bell,
and nothing happened, so I didn't want to waste another charge getting
it wrong.

